<div class="eyebrow">GUIDE / TRAVEL</div><h1>Jet lag and crossing time zones</h1><p class="lead">Crossing several time zones can temporarily disrupt sleep and alertness.</p><p class="notice">WorldScope editorial team · Updated November 2026 · <a href="/info/methodology/">Methodology</a></p><h2>Before and after a trip</h2><p>Check your arrival date and local time, allow time to rest, and consider gradually adjusting your sleep schedule before departure. At your destination, local daylight and regular sleep times may help your body adapt. Experiences differ by person, route and travel conditions.</p><h2>Use local arrival time</h2><p>Enter the departure local time, destination and elapsed flight duration in the converter. Remember that the calculator does not account for delays, connections or changes to your itinerary.</p><p>This general information is not medical advice. Ask a qualified clinician about persistent sleep issues or individual treatment. See the <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/yellow-book/hcp/travel-air-sea/jet-lag-disorder.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">CDC Yellow Book guidance on jet lag</a> for more detail.</p><p><a class="button" href="/#planning">Open travel converter ↗</a></p>
